  <h1>Property cleanout partner for professionals near Lowell, Michigan</h1>
  <p class="lede smaller">
    When a property has to be emptied before it can be listed, let, sold or closed on, somebody has to actually
    clear it. RYDJA is the crew for that part &mdash; scoped from photographs, quoted before anything is booked,
    and handled by the owner-operator who priced it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most of this work reaches us second-hand. An agent with a listing full of furniture. A property manager with
    four days between tenants. An estate-sale company that has sold what will sell and needs the rest gone. An
    attorney&rsquo;s client who has inherited a house two hundred miles from where they live. In every case the
    person calling is not the person whose property it is, and what they need from a vendor is the same: a price
    they can pass on, a date they can rely on, and no surprises to explain afterwards.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the whole reason this page exists. If RYDJA is useful to you once, it is worth knowing where to find us
    the next time, and this is the page to keep or forward.
  </p>

  <p class="cta-inline">
    <a class="button" href="/quote?service=<%= encodeURIComponent('Rental / property turnover') %>">Request a property cleanout quote</a>
    <% if (businessPhone) { %>
      <span class="or-call">or call RYDJA on <%- include('partials/call-text') %></span>
    <% } %>
  </p>

  <h2>Who sends us work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Realtors and listing agents</strong> &mdash; getting a property empty, swept and photographable before it goes live</li>
    <li><strong>Property managers</strong> &mdash; turnovers between tenants, on the turnover&rsquo;s own clock</li>
    <li><strong>Landlords and owners</strong> &mdash; single units, houses, or several at once</li>
    <li><strong>Real-estate investors</strong> &mdash; a property bought as-is and full of somebody else&rsquo;s things</li>
    <li><strong>Estate-sale companies and auctioneers</strong> &mdash; clearing what is left once the sale is over</li>
    <li><strong>Senior move managers and downsizing professionals</strong> &mdash; the part of a move that is not moving</li>
    <li><strong>Probate, estate and elder-law professionals</strong> &mdash; a vendor to hand a client who has a house to empty</li>
    <li><strong>Restoration companies</strong> &mdash; contents removal and debris hauling alongside your own work</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we clear</h2>
  <p>
    All of it is quoted the same way and can be combined into one job on one property rather than split across
    several vendors.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/estate-cleanouts">Estate and inherited-property cleanouts</a> &mdash; a whole house, garage, basement, attic, barn and outbuildings</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/rental-property-cleanouts">Rental turnovers and tenant move-out cleanouts</a> &mdash; abandoned furniture and household contents</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/cleanouts">Garage, basement, outbuilding and storage-unit cleanouts</a> &mdash; a whole space back to empty</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/furniture-appliance-removal">Furniture and appliance removal</a> &mdash; including single heavy items down a stair</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/junk-removal">Junk removal and debris hauling</a> &mdash; one load or several</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/yard-cleanup">Yard and outdoor debris clearing</a> &mdash; brush, storm damage, outdoor clutter before a showing</li>
    <li><a class="text-link" href="/light-demolition">Light demolition where it is appropriate</a> &mdash; sheds, playsets, fencing, shelving; non-structural only</li>
    <li><strong>Pre-listing property clearing</strong> &mdash; the combination of the above that gets a property ready to photograph</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How working with us actually goes</h2>
  <ol class="area-steps">
    <li>
      <strong>You send photographs</strong> &mdash; yours, the owner&rsquo;s, or whoever did the walkthrough.
      Rooms, outbuildings, and the route out to where a truck can park. No site visit to schedule, which is the
      step that normally costs you two days.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We scope it and send a price</strong> &mdash; based on volume, access, labour and disposal. One
      number, with what it covers written down. Nothing is booked and nothing is owed at this stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You or your client approves it</strong> &mdash; from a phone, wherever anybody happens to be.
      Approval is the customer&rsquo;s, always: we do not start work on a quote nobody has agreed to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We schedule it</strong> &mdash; if we have proposed a time with the quote, confirming it books the
      job. If that slot does not fit your closing, listing or turnover date, ask and we will work to yours where
      we can, or tell you plainly if we cannot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We clear it and tell you it is done</strong> &mdash; loaded, hauled, disposed of, swept. Photographs
      of the finished space where we have them, which is usually what you need for your own file.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why professionals keep us on the list</h2>
  <h3>Fast photo-based scoping</h3>
  <p>
    You do not have to be at the property and neither do we. A set of photographs from a phone is enough to price
    almost any cleanout, which means a number can be in your hands the same day you asked rather than after a
    site-visit appointment that suited a vendor&rsquo;s calendar.
  </p>

  <h3>One point of contact</h3>
  <p>
    RYDJA is owner-operated. The person who reads your photographs, prices the job and answers your text is the
    person on site doing it. There is no dispatcher in the middle and no account manager to be handed between.
  </p>

  <h3>A quote your client approves, not a running meter</h3>
  <p>
    The price is agreed before the work starts. If a property turns out to be materially bigger than the
    photographs showed &mdash; an attic nobody opened, a crawl space nobody mentioned &mdash; we stop and come back
    to you rather than quietly adding to the bill. The number that was approved is the number unless somebody
    agrees to a different one.
  </p>

  <h3>Documentation where it exists</h3>
  <p>
    Every request carries its own work reference, which is the thing to quote on an invoice or in a message about
    it. We send photographs of the finished space where we have them. We do not manufacture before-and-after
    marketing out of a client&rsquo;s property.
  </p>

  <h3>Recovery rather than straight to landfill</h3>
  <p>
    Where it is practical and the owner has authorized it, usable material is separated for donation, recycling,
    scrap or resale instead of being buried. Worth knowing if your client cares what happens to the contents, and
    on some jobs it affects the price.
  </p>

  <h3>Vendor paperwork on request</h3>
  <p>
    A W-9 and the usual vendor onboarding paperwork are available on request &mdash; ask and we will send them to
    you directly. They are not published on this page, and nor is anything else that belongs in a file rather than
    on a website.
  </p>

  <div class="panel notice">
    <h3>What we don&rsquo;t claim, and won&rsquo;t</h3>
    <p>
      So you are not relying on something nobody checked: RYDJA does not claim to be insured, bonded, licensed,
      accredited or certified on this page, because none of that has been separately verified. If your process
      requires a certificate of insurance or a licence number before you can place work with a vendor, ask us
      directly and we will tell you honestly what we can and cannot produce. We would rather lose the job than
      have you discover the answer later.
    </p>
    <p>
      We are also not attorneys, an eviction service, process servers, appraisers, a cleaning company or a
      structural demolition contractor. Where a job needs one of those, that is what we will tell you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What we need from you on a job</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Authority</strong> &mdash; confirmation that the owner or an authorized agent has the right to have the contents removed</li>
    <li><strong>Access</strong> &mdash; lockbox, code, key handover, or somebody meeting us there; and whether a truck and trailer can get close</li>
    <li><strong>A clear statement of what stays</strong> &mdash; appliances included in a let, anything the owner is keeping, anything already sold</li>
    <li><strong>The date</strong> &mdash; the closing, listing, inspection or turnover date, so we can tell you honestly whether it is workable</li>
    <li><strong>Known hazards</strong> &mdash; anything on the exclusion list, so we can arrange around it rather than discover it</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="panel notice">
    <h3>What we can&rsquo;t take on</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>No hazardous waste, asbestos, chemicals, fuels, solvents, paint, propane or batteries</li>
      <li>No biohazards, regulated medical waste, sewage, or contaminated or soiled material</li>
      <li>No pest, mould or environmental remediation</li>
      <li>No cleaning, decorating, repairs or surface preparation &mdash; we clear and sweep</li>
      <li>Nothing structural: no building demolition, no load-bearing work, no excavation</li>
      <li>No gas, plumbing or hard-wired electrical disconnection &mdash; a licensed trade first</li>
      <li>No legal, probate, title, eviction or tenancy advice, and no determination of abandonment</li>
      <li>We do not appraise, value or purchase property contents</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you are unsure whether a particular job fits, send photographs and ask before you commit a date to anybody.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>

  <h3>Can I get a price without anyone visiting the property?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes &mdash; that is the normal route. Photographs of the rooms and buildings involved, plus the route out, are
    enough to price almost any cleanout. Send them through the <a class="text-link" href="/quote">quote form</a>
    and the price comes back to you.
  </p>

  <h3>Can my client approve and pay rather than me?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Tell us who the quote should go to and that is who receives it and approves it. Equally, if you are
    arranging and settling it yourself, that is fine too &mdash; just say which when you send the request.
  </p>

  <h3>Can you work to a closing, listing or turnover date?</h3>
  <p>
    Send the date with the request. You will get a straight answer about whether it is workable rather than an
    encouraging one. A missed closing is a far bigger problem for you than a job we turned down.
  </p>

  <h3>Do you take on more than one property at a time?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, and more than one unit in a building. Photograph each one separately so the quote covers all of them, and
    say whether they have to be done together or can be staged over a week or two.
  </p>

  <h3>Will you buy the contents?</h3>
  <p>
    No. RYDJA is not a buyer, dealer or appraiser and will not make an offer on what is in a property. If you
    think there is something genuinely valuable in there, get it looked at by a specialist before we arrive. We
    are the people who clear what is left afterwards.
  </p>

  <h3>Do you work outside the named service area?</h3>
  <p>
    Sometimes, and larger jobs travel further than small ones. A whole-property clearance is often worth a longer
    drive where a single-item pickup is not. See the
    <a class="text-link" href="/service-area">service area</a> for where we work and ask about anything just
    outside it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where we work</h2>
  <p>
    RYDJA serves <%= serviceArea %>, and <%= legalEntity %> is the entity behind the brand. Every job is quoted
    individually from photographs &mdash; there are no packages, no per-unit rates and no minimum contract to sign
    before you can send one over.
  </p>

